cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use or refer to this game, please cite the software itself (below).
  The paper listed under `references` inspired the game but is not implemented
  by it: cite that paper for the theory of reconstructive-spectrometer design.
title: "Fleeting Light (流光): a playable chaotic reconstructive spectrometer"
abstract: >-
  A browser game in which the player places circular scatterers inside a leaky
  rectangular cavity to shape the dwell-time distribution of a handful of
  photons. Each photon is one sample of that distribution; the detector's N
  layers are the ideal distribution cut into N equal-probability blocks, each
  guarding one time window. A layer is stripped when a photon arrives inside
  its window, and the run fails the moment a window closes empty. The spectral
  resolution shown on the victory screen is not a preset difficulty target: it
  is inferred from the players own arrival times through the spectral
  correlation C(dnu) = |FT{p(t)}|^2. Every rule in the game is computed rather
  than tuned for game feel. Its scope is deliberately narrow: it demonstrates
  how inverse design reshapes the distribution of optical path lengths, and
  does not implement the Fisher-information bound Tr[G+] or its decomposition
  into a spectral correlation length and a mean transmittance, which is where
  the optimal design of such a spectrometer is actually settled.
